```javascript
const { EventEmitter } = require('events');
const stream = require('stream');

class DataMerger extends EventEmitter {
    constructor(maxBufferSize) {
        super();
        this.buffer = [];
        this.maxBufferSize = maxBufferSize;
    }

    addDataSource(dataSource) {
        dataSource.on('data', (chunk) => {
            if (this.buffer.length >= this.maxBufferSize) {
                console.error("Buffer overflow, dropping chunk");
                return;
            }

            try {
                // Add your own logic for handling and merging data chunks here
                this.buffer.push(chunk);

                if (this.buffer.length === this.maxBufferSize) {
                    this.emit('data', this.buffer);
                    this.buffer = [];
                }
            } catch (error) {
                console.error("Error merging data:", error);
            }
        });

        dataSource.on('end', () => {
            if (this.buffer.length > 0) {
                this.emit('data', this.buffer);
                this.buffer = [];
            }

            this.emit('end');
        });
    }
}
```

This code creates a DataMerger class that extends Node.js's EventEmitter, allowing it to emit events when data is ready for processing or when all sources have ended. The addDataSource method sets up event listeners on the provided data source object, which should be an instance of stream.Readable.

The 'data' event listener checks if adding a new chunk would exceed the maximum buffer size and drops it if necessary. It then attempts to merge the new chunk with existing buffered data, handling any errors that occur during this process. If the buffer is full after adding a chunk, it emits a 'data' event with the current buffer contents and clears the buffer for the next batch of chunks.

The 'end' event listener checks if there are any remaining chunks in the buffer when a data source ends, emitting a 'data' event if necessary before clearing the buffer. It then emits an 'end' event to signal that all sources have ended. 

