['Here is a basic implementation of the problem using Java. This code does not include all the features you mentioned like logging mechanism, error handling etc. It only provides a Union-Find data structure to manage disjoint sets efficiently. 

```java
import java.util.*;

class Transaction {
    String id;
    // Additional metadata can be added here

    public Transaction(String id) {
        this.id = id;
    }
}

public class DisjointSetUnion {
    private Map<Transaction, Transaction> parent;
    private Map<Transaction, Integer> rank;

    public DisjointSetUnion() {
        parent = new HashMap<>();
        rank = new HashMap<>();
    }

    public void makeSet(Transaction transaction) {
        parent.put(transaction, transaction);
        rank.put(transaction, 0);
    }

    public Transaction find(Transaction transaction) {
        if (parent.get(transaction) != transaction) {
            parent.put(transaction, find(parent.get(transaction))); // Path compression
        }
        return parent.get(transaction);
    }

    public void union(Transaction tx1, Transaction tx2) {
        Transaction rootX = find(tx1);
        Transaction rootY = find(tx2);

        if (rootX == rootY) { // Already in the same set
            return;
        } else {
            if (rank.get(rootX) > rank.get(rootY)) {
                parent.put(rootY, rootX);
            } else if (rank.get(rootX) < rank.get(rootY)) {
                parent.put(rootX, rootY);
            } else {
                parent.put(rootY, rootX);
                rank.put(rootX, rank.get(rootX) + 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a disjoint set data structure using Union-Find algorithm which is efficient for large number of transactions and provides low memory footprint. It also includes path compression to optimize the find operation. 

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not include all the features you mentioned like logging mechanism, error handling etc. You would need to add these features based on your specific requirements. Also, please consider using a profiler or performance testing tools to identify bottlenecks in your system before making any optimization decisions.
']

['Here is an example of how you might structure such a Java method using NIO (Non-blocking I/O) for handling multiple remote clients. This code does not include all the features mentioned in your request due to its complexity and length, but it should give you a good starting point. 

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;

public class Server {
    private Selector selector;
    private ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(256);

    public void startServer() throws IOException {
        this.selector = Selector.open();
        ServerSocketChannel serverSocket = ServerSocketChannel.open();
        serverSocket.bind(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 5454));
        serverSocket.configureBlocking(false);
        serverSocket.register(this.selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);

        while (true) {
            this.selector.select();
            Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = this.selector.selectedKeys();
            Iterator<SelectionKey> iter = selectedKeys.iterator();

            while (iter.hasNext()) {
                SelectionKey key = iter.next();

                if (key.isAcceptable()) {
                    register(serverSocket);
                }

                if (key.isReadable()) {
                    readDataFromClient((SocketChannel) key.channel());
                }

                if (key.isWritable() && key.attachment() != null) {
                    writeDataToClient(buffer, key);
                }

                iter.remove();
            }
        }
    }

    private void register(ServerSocketChannel serverSocket) throws IOException {
        SocketChannel client = serverSocket.accept();
        client.configureBlocking(false);
        client.register(this.selector, SelectionKey.OP_READ);
    }

    protected void readDataFromClient(SocketChannel client) throws IOException {
        this.buffer.clear();
        client.read(this.buffer);

        if (new String(this.buffer.array()).trim().equals("")) {
            System.out.println("Client dropped connection...");
            client.close();
        } else {
            processDataFromClient(client, this.buffer);
        }
    }

    protected void writeDataToClient(ByteBuffer buffer, SelectionKey key) throws IOException {
        SocketChannel client = (SocketChannel) key.channel();
        client.write(buffer);
        key.interestOps(SelectionKey.OP_READ);
    }

    private void processDataFromClient(SocketChannel client, ByteBuffer buffer) {
        // Process data from the client here...
    }
}
```
