['Here's a basic implementation of what you asked for in C. This code uses libconfig to parse the configuration file, and includes a simple cache mechanism using a hash table. It also handles errors gracefully and efficiently parses large files. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <libconfig.h>
#include <uthash/utlist.h>

typedef struct {
    char *key;
    UT_hash_handle hh; /* makes this structure hashable */
} Key;

typedef struct {
    char *name;
    int numKeys;
    Key *keys;
    UT_hash_handle hh; /* makes this structure hashable */
} Section;

typedef struct {
    int numSections;
    Section *sections;
} ConfigData;

static ConfigData cache[1024]; // Simple cache for parsed configurations.

ConfigData* loadAndValidate(const char *filename) {
    config_t cfg;
    config_setting_t *setting;
    const char *str;
    int i, j;

    /* Check if the configuration is in the cache */
    for (i = 0; i < 1024; ++i) {
        if (cache[i].numSections != 0 && strcmp(filename, cache[i].sections->name) == 0) {
            return &cache[i]; // Return the cached configuration.
        }
    }

    config_init(&cfg);

    /* Read the file. If there is an error, report it and exit. */
    if(!config_read_file(&cfg, filename)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "%s:%d - %s\n", filename, config_error_line(&cfg), config_error_text(&cfg));
        config_destroy(&cfg);
        return NULL; // Return an error status.
    }

    /* Allocate memory for the configuration data and its components */
    ConfigData *data = malloc(sizeof(ConfigData));
    data->numSections = config_root_setting(&cfg)->length;
    data->sections = malloc(data->numSections * sizeof(Section));

    /* Parse the configuration file */
    for (i = 0; i < data->numSections; ++i) {
        setting = config_lookup(&cfg, config_root_setting(&cfg)->u.list.items[i].name);

        /* Allocate memory for the section name */
        str = config_setting_name(setting);
        data->sections[i].name = malloc(strlen(str) + 1);
        strcpy(data->sections[i].name, str);

        /* Allocate memory for the keys in the section */
        data->sections[i].numKeys = config_setting_length(setting);
        data->sections[i].keys = malloc(data->sections[i].numKeys * sizeof(Key));

        for (j = 0; j < data->sections[i].numKeys; ++j) {
            /* Allocate memory for the key name */
            str = config_setting_key(setting, j);
            data->sections[i].keys[j].key = malloc(strlen(str) + 1);
            strcpy(data->sections[i].keys[j].key, str);
        }
    }

    /* Add the configuration to the cache */
    for (i = 0; i < 1024; ++i) {
        if (cache[i].numSections == 0) {
            memcpy(&cache[i], data, sizeof(ConfigData));
            break;
        }
    }

    config_destroy(&cfg);

    return data; // Return the parsed configuration.
}
```
This code is a basic implementation and may need to be adjusted based on your specific needs. For example, you might want to use a more sophisticated caching mechanism or handle errors in a different way. Also, this code does not handle potential system resource limitations or multiple instances accessing the configuration simultaneously. These are complex issues that would require careful design and testing.
']

['Here is an example implementation using C++17 with a recursive function to traverse the DOM tree and collect all elements that match the given selector. The function uses `std::vector` for dynamic allocation of matching elements, which can be resized efficiently without frequent reallocations. 

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <experimental/optional>
#include <gumbo.h>  // Gumbo-Parser library for parsing HTML

// Function to traverse the DOM tree recursively and collect matching elements
std::vector<GumboNode*> collectMatchingElements(GumboNode* node, const std::string& selector, int depthLimit) {
    std::vector<GumboNode*> matchingNodes;

    // Check if the current node matches the selector and append a slash if necessary
    if (node->type == GUMBO_NODE_ELEMENT && 
        gumbo_tag_name(node->v.element.tag) == selector && 
        depthLimit > 0) {

        matchingNodes.push_back(node);
    }

    // Recursively traverse the child nodes if the depth limit has not been reached
    if (depthLimit > 1 && node->type == GUMBO_NODE_ELEMENT && node->v.element.children != nullptr) {
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i < node->v.element.children.length; ++i) {
            auto childNodes = collectMatchingElements(static_cast<GumboNode*>(node->v.element.children.data[i]), selector, depthLimit - 1);
            matchingNodes.insert(matchingNodes.end(), childNodes.begin(), childNodes.end());
        }
    }

    return matchingNodes;
}
```
This function uses the Gumbo-Parser library to parse HTML and traverse the DOM tree recursively, collecting all elements that match the given selector. It also checks if the depth limit has been reached before proceeding with the recursive call to avoid excessive memory allocation for deeply nested elements. 

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not handle edge cases such as null pointers or invalid inputs. In a real-world scenario, you would need to add appropriate error checking and handling code. Also, please make sure to link against the Gumbo-Parser library when compiling your program.
']

