['Here is an example implementation in C# using `System.IO` for file handling and `System.Security.Cryptography` for SHA256 hashing. This solution assumes that each line of the input file can be read into memory as a string, which may not be true if lines are too large or if there is an external data source involved.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class FileProcessor
{
    private readonly string _inputFilePath;
    private readonly string _outputFilePath;

    public FileProcessor(string inputFilePath, string outputFilePath)
    {
        _inputFilePath = inputFilePath;
        _outputFilePath = outputFilePath;
    }

    public void Process()
    {
        try
        {
            using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(_inputFilePath))
            {
                using (StreamWriter writer = new StreamWriter(_outputFilePath))
                {
                    string line;
                    while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
                    {
                        try
                        {
                            var transformedLine = Transform(line);
                            writer.WriteLine(transformedLine);
                        }
                        catch (Exception ex)
                        {
                            // Log the error and continue with the next line
                            Console.Error.WriteLine($"Failed to process line: {ex}");
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        catch (IOException ex)
        {
            // Log the error and rethrow for further handling
            Console.Error.WriteLine($"I/O exception occurred: {ex}");
            throw;
        }
    }

    private string Transform(string line)
    {
        using (SHA256 sha256 = SHA256.Create())
        {
            byte[] hashBytes = sha256.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(line));
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            foreach (byte b in hashBytes)
                sb.AppendFormat("{0:x2}", b);

            return line + "," + sb;
        }
    }
}
```
This solution reads the input file line by line, transforms each line using a `Transform` method that calculates and appends a SHA-256 hash to it. If an error occurs during transformation or writing to the output file, it logs the error and continues with the next line. 

The use of `using` blocks ensures that resources are properly disposed even if exceptions occur. This is important for preventing memory leaks and other resource issues.

This solution assumes that each line can be read into memory as a string, which may not be true if lines are too large or if there is an external data source involved. If this assumption does not hold, the solution would need to be adjusted accordingly. 

The logging infrastructure is abstracted away in this example by simply writing errors to `Console.Error`. In a real-world scenario, you might want to use a proper logging library or framework that integrates seamlessly with your existing infrastructure.
']

