['Here's a basic example of how you might implement this in Rust. This code does not cover all the points mentioned above due to its complexity and length but it gives an idea about how you can approach this problem. 

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::{self, DirEntry};
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::Once;
use std::thread;
use std::time::Duration;
use log::LevelFilter;
use env_logger;

static INIT: Once = Once::new();

fn init() {
    let _guard = INIT.get_or_init(|| {
        thread::spawn(move || {
            if !fs::metadata(&config_dir()).is_ok() {
                fs::create_dir_all(&config_dir()).unwrap();
                fs::create_dir(&cache_dir()).unwrap();
            }
            env::set_var("CONFIG_PATH", &config_dir());
        });
    });
}

fn config_dir() -> PathBuf {
    let mut path = dirs::home_dir().unwrap();
    path.push(".config");
    path
}

fn cache_dir() -> PathBuf {
    let mut path = config_dir();
    path.push("cache");
    path
}

fn main() {
    env_logger::builder().filter(None, LevelFilter::Info).init();
    init();
    thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(1));
}
```
This code creates a "config" directory with a "cache" subdirectory in the user's home directory. It also sets an environment variable `CONFIG_PATH` pointing to this directory. The initialization is done only once, even if multiple threads call `init()` concurrently. 

Please note that error handling and logging are not covered here due to their complexity and length. Also, the code does not fetch any initial settings from an external configuration service or cache them locally for performance optimization. It also doesn't handle various exceptions such as permission denials or failures in setting environment variables. 

