['Here's a basic example of how you could implement this in Java using NIO2 (New I/O 2) which includes non-blocking IO operations. This code does not include all the features mentioned in your request, but it should give you an idea of how to start implementing them.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.logging.FileHandler;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
import java.util.logging.SimpleFormatter;

public class Server {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(Server.class.getName());
    private Selector selector;
    private ConcurrentHashMap<SocketChannel, ByteBuffer> sessions = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void startServer() throws IOException {
        FileHandler fh = new FileHandler("serverLogs.log", true);
        logger.addHandler(fh);
        SimpleFormatter formatter = new SimpleFormatter();  
        fh.setFormatter(formatter); 

        selector = Selector.open();
        ServerSocketChannel serverSocket = ServerSocketChannel.open();
        serverSocket.bind(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 5454));
        serverSocket.configureBlocking(false);
        serverSocket.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);

        while (true) {
            selector.select();
            Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = selector.selectedKeys();
            Iterator<SelectionKey> iter = selectedKeys.iterator();

            while (iter.hasNext()) {
                SelectionKey key = iter.next();

                if (key.isAcceptable()) {
                    register(serverSocket);
                } else if (key.isReadable()) {
                    readDataFromClient((SocketChannel) key.channel());
                }
                iter.remove();
            }
        }
    }

    private void register(ServerSocketChannel serverSocket) throws IOException {
        SocketChannel client = serverSocket.accept();
        client.configureBlocking(false);
        ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect(1024);
        sessions.put(client, buffer);
        client.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_READ, buffer);
    }

    private void readDataFromClient(SocketChannel client) throws IOException {
        ByteBuffer buffer = sessions.get(client);
        int bytesRead;

        while ((bytesRead = client.read(buffer)) > 0) {
            logger.info("Received data from client: " + new String(buffer.array(), 0, bytesRead));
            buffer.clear();
        }

        if (bytesRead == -1) {
            client.close();
            sessions.remove(client);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        new Server().startServer();
    }
}
```
This is a very basic example and does not include all the features you requested, such as connection pooling, health checking, or logging. Implementing these features would require a more complex design and additional classes. Also, this code doesn't handle exceptions properly which are essential for production-level code. 

Please note that NIO is a low-level API and it can be quite difficult to use correctly. If you need high-level abstractions like connection pooling or health checking, consider using libraries such as Netty or MINA. These libraries provide these features out of the box and are designed for production use.
']

