['Here is a basic implementation of such a method using Java's built-in serialization mechanism. This example uses Google's Gson library for JSON deserialization, but you can replace it with any other suitable library or custom code. 

```java
import com.google.gson.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Type;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(Deserializer.class);

    // Cache for deserialized objects
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    // Gson instance with custom deserialization strategy
    private Gson gson = new GsonBuilder()
        .setObjectSizeCalculator(new ObjectSizeCalculator())
        .registerTypeAdapterFactory(new RecoveringDeserializerModifier())
        .create();

    public <T> T deserialize(byte[] data, Class<T> clazz) {
        String hash = DigestUtils.sha256Hex(data);

        // Check cache first
        if (cache.containsKey(hash)) {
            return clazz.cast(cache.get(hash));
        }

        T object;
        try {
            object = gson.fromJson(new String(data), clazz);
        } catch (JsonSyntaxException e) {
            logger.warn("Failed to deserialize data", e);

            // Attempt recovery by skipping invalid fields
            Gson recoveringGson = new GsonBuilder()
                .registerTypeAdapterFactory(new RecoveringDeserializerModifier())
                .create();
            object = recoveringGson.fromJson(new String(data), clazz);
        }

        // Cache the deserialized object
        cache.put(hash, object);

        return object;
    }

    private static class ObjectSizeCalculator implements com.google.gson.ObjectSizeCalculator {
        @Override
        public long sizeOf(Object obj) {
            // Implement a custom strategy for estimating the memory footprint of objects
            // This can be based on the actual object graph, or just its size in bytes
            return 0;
        }
    }

    private static class RecoveringDeserializerModifier implements TypeAdapterFactory {
        @Override
        public <T> TypeAdapter<T> create(Gson gson, TypeToken<T> type) {
            TypeAdapter<T> delegate = gson.getDelegateAdapter(this, type);

            return new TypeAdapter<T>() {
                @Override
                public void write(JsonWriter out, T value) throws IOException {
                    delegate.write(out, value);
                }

                @Override
                public T read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {
                    try {
                        return delegate.read(in);
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        logger.warn("Failed to deserialize field", e);

                        // Skip the invalid field and continue with the next one
                        in.skipValue();
                        return null;
                    }
                }
            };
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a combination of caching, custom deserialization strategies, and error recovery mechanisms to meet your requirements. However, it's important to note that this is just a basic example and may need further adjustments based on the specific requirements of your project. For instance, you might want to use a more efficient caching strategy or integrate with external services in a different way depending on your needs.
']

