#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct sensor_data {
    int id;
    float temperature;
    double timestamp;
    int status; // active or inactive
} sensor_data;

typedef struct sensor_array {
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t size;
    sensor_data *data;
} sensor_array;

sensor_array* create_sensor_array(size_t initial_capacity) {
    sensor_array *arr = malloc(sizeof(sensor_array));
    arr->capacity = initial_capacity;
    arr->size = 0;
    arr->data = malloc(initial_capacity * sizeof(sensor_data));
    pthread_mutex_init(&arr->lock, NULL);
    return arr;
}

void add_to_array(sensor_array *arr, sensor_data data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&arr->lock);

    if (arr->size == arr->capacity) {
        // Double the capacity when full
        arr->capacity *= 2;
        arr->data = realloc(arr->data, arr->capacity * sizeof(sensor_data));
    }

    arr->data[arr->size++] = data;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&arr->lock);
}

void free_array(sensor_array *arr) {
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&arr->lock);
    free(arr->data);
    free(arr);
}
```
This code uses a dynamic array to store the sensor data. When the array is full, it doubles its capacity using `realloc`. The array is protected by a mutex to ensure thread safety. 

For handling varying data rates and sizes, you might want to consider implementing a buffer that stores incoming data until enough has been collected to process it. This could be implemented as a circular buffer or a queue. For concurrent access from multiple threads without causing data corruption or race conditions, the mutex lock is used. 

The trade-offs between using a linked list and an array for managing sensor data structures include cache locality (arrays are more cache friendly), thread safety (linked lists can be made thread safe with locks but arrays are inherently thread safe), and ease of implementation (linked lists require more code to manage the links, while arrays are simpler). 

To balance between initial memory usage and peak memory consumption during high-load conditions, you could implement a dynamic resizing strategy. For example, when the array is full, double its size instead of increasing it by a fixed amount. This can help reduce fragmentation and maintain optimal access times. However, this approach may lead to more frequent reallocations and copying of data, which could impact performance under high load conditions. 

Please note that this code does not include error checking for simplicity. In a real-world application, you would want to add appropriate error handling.
