['This task is quite complex and involves multiple aspects such as process management, network monitoring, logging, environment variable handling, thread safety, etc. Here's a simplified version of how you might implement this in C using POSIX threads (pthread) for concurrency and pcap library for packet capture. Please note that the actual implementation would be much more complex and require a deep understanding of network programming, system calls, and synchronization primitives.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <pcap.h>

// Global variables for synchronization and shared resources
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int globalHealthScore = 0;
char* anomalyPacketId = NULL;

void* monitorNetwork(void *arg) {
    char errbuf[PCAP_ERRBUF_SIZE];
    pcap_t *handle = pcap_open_live("eth0", BUFSIZ, 1 /* promiscuous mode */, -1 /* read timeout */, errbuf);

    if (handle == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Couldn't open device: %s\n", errbuf);
        return NULL;
    }

    struct pcap_pkthdr header;  // The packet header
    const u_char *packet;      // The actual packet data

    while (1) {
        packet = pcap_next(handle, &header);

        if (packet == NULL) continue;

        // Process the packet and update globalHealthScore and anomalyPacketId
        // ...

        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        // Update shared resources here
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }

    pcap_close(handle);
}

int executeAndMonitorProcess(char* command) {
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid < 0) {
        perror("fork failed");
        return 0;
    } else if (pid == 0) { // Child process
        system(command);
        exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
    } else { // Parent process
        int status = 0;

        pthread_t threadId;
        pthread_create(&threadId, NULL, monitorNetwork, NULL);

        waitpid(pid, &status, 0); // Wait for child to finish

        if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
            return WEXITSTATUS(status) == 0;
        } else {
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simplified version of the code. The actual implementation would involve more complex logic for network monitoring, error handling, logging, and environment variable management. Also, please note that this code does not handle race conditions or deadlocks as it's beyond the scope of this task. You need to use appropriate synchronization primitives like mutexes, semaphores, condition variables etc., based on your specific requirements.
