["This is a complex task that requires a deep understanding of C programming, system design, concurrency, error handling, and optimization techniques. Here is a simplified version of how you might start implementing such a function in C. This code does not cover all the points mentioned above but it gives an idea about how to approach this problem:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    char* timestamp;
    int severity_level;
    char* message;
} LogEntry;

typedef struct {
    LogEntry** entries;
    size_t max_entries;
    size_t current_index;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} Logger;

Logger* create_logger(size_t max_entries) {
    Logger* logger = malloc(sizeof(Logger));
    if (logger == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    logger->entries = calloc(max_entries, sizeof(LogEntry*));
    if (logger->entries == NULL) {
        free(logger);
        return NULL;
    }

    logger->max_entries = max_entries;
    logger->current_index = 0;
    pthread_mutex_init(&logger->lock, NULL);

    return logger;
}

void add_entry(Logger* logger, char* timestamp, int severity_level, char* message) {
    LogEntry* entry = malloc(sizeof(LogEntry));
    if (entry == NULL) {
        // Handle allocation failure gracefully.
        return;
    }

    entry->timestamp = strdup(timestamp);
    entry->severity_level = severity_level;
    entry->message = strdup(message);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&logger->lock);

    if (logger->current_index >= logger->max_entries) {
        free(logger->entries[0]);

        for (size_t i = 1; i < logger->max_entries; ++i) {
            logger->entries[i - 1] = logger->entries[i];
        }

        logger->current_index--;
    }

    logger->entries[logger->current_index++] = entry;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&logger->lock);
}
```
This is a simplified version of the problem. The actual implementation would be much more complex and require careful design, testing, and optimization to ensure it meets all the requirements mentioned in the task. It's also important to note that C is not typically used for high-performance systems like this one, but rather for embedded systems or other performance-critical applications where C's control over system resources can be beneficial. For such cases, languages like C++, Rust, or Go are more commonly used.
