['Here is a simplified version of what your code might look like. This is just a basic example and does not include all the features you mentioned. It's important to note that this is a complex task that requires careful design and implementation. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;

class Config {
    private Map<String, String> keyValuePairs = new HashMap<>();
    private int priorityLevel;
    private Instant lastAccessedTimestamp;

    // getters and setters for the fields
}

public class ConfigurationManager {
    private static final int MAX_CONFIGS = 5000;
    private Map<String, WeakReference<Config>> configMap = new HashMap<>();
    private PriorityQueue<Config> priorityQueue = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b.getPriorityLevel() - a.getPriorityLevel());

    public synchronized void addOrUpdate(String key, Config config) throws Exception {
        if (configMap.size() >= MAX_CONFIGS) {
            throw new Exception("Maximum number of configurations exceeded");
        }

        WeakReference<Config> weakRef = configMap.get(key);
        if (weakRef != null && weakRef.get() != null) {
            priorityQueue.remove(weakRef.get());
        }

        configMap.put(key, new WeakReference<>(config));
        priorityQueue.add(config);
    }

    public synchronized void remove(String key) {
        WeakReference<Config> weakRef = configMap.remove(key);
        if (weakRef != null && weakRef.get() != null) {
            priorityQueue.remove(weakRef.get());
        }
    }

    public synchronized Config getByKey(String key) {
        WeakReference<Config> weakRef = configMap.get(key);
        return weakRef == null ? null : weakRef.get();
    }

    public synchronized List<Config> getByPriorityLevel(int priorityLevel) {
        List<Config> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Config config : priorityQueue) {
            if (config.getPriorityLevel() == priorityLevel) {
                result.add(config);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```
This code provides a basic implementation of the ConfigurationManager class, which manages configurations in a map and a priority queue. It uses weak references to allow garbage collection when configurations are no longer strongly referenced. The addOrUpdate method checks if adding another configuration would exceed the maximum number of configurations, throwing an exception if it does.

