['Here's a basic implementation of your requirements using C with POSIX threads for thread safety and qsort for sorting tasks by priority. This is a simplified version and doesn't include all the error checking you might need in a real-world application, but it should give you an idea of how to approach this problem:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Task {
    int id;
    int priority;
    char* description;
} Task;

Task** tasks = NULL;
int taskCount = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void addTask(int id, int priority) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; ++i) {
        if (tasks[i]->id == id) {
            printf("Error: Duplicate ID\n");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return;
        }
    }

    Task* newTask = malloc(sizeof(Task));
    if (newTask == NULL) {
        perror("malloc failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    newTask->id = id;
    newTask->priority = priority;
    newTask->description = malloc(sizeof(char) * (strlen("project") + 100));
    if (newTask->description == NULL) {
        perror("malloc failed");
        free(newTask);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    tasks = realloc(tasks, sizeof(Task*) * (taskCount + 1));
    if (tasks == NULL) {
        perror("realloc failed");
        free(newTask->description);
        free(newTask);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    tasks[taskCount++] = newTask;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

int compareTasks(const void* a, const void* b) {
    return ((Task*)b)->priority - ((Task*)a)->priority;
}

void sortTasks() {
    qsort(tasks, taskCount, sizeof(Task*), compareTasks);
}

void freeAllMemory() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; ++i) {
        free(tasks[i]->description);
        free(tasks[i]);
    }

    free(tasks);
    tasks = NULL;
    taskCount = 0;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```

This code uses a simple array of pointers to `Task` structures, which is efficient for insertion but less efficient for sorting. If you need to frequently add and remove tasks while also frequently sorting them, it might be more efficient to use a linked list or a balanced binary search tree instead. However, these data structures are more complex to implement and can have higher overhead due to the additional pointers.

The `qsort` function used for sorting is a good general-purpose sorting algorithm that has good performance characteristics for many types of data. If you know more about your specific data or workload, you might be able to choose a more efficient sorting algorithm. For example, if the tasks are already mostly sorted and only occasionally need to be resorted, you could use an insertion sort instead.
']

