['Here is an example implementation in Java using ConcurrentHashMap for Union-Find data structure and AtomicLong for logging milliseconds taken for each disjointness check. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class TransactionProcessor {
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> parent = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private AtomicLong disjointnessCheckTime = new AtomicLong(0);

    public void processTransactions(Stream<Transaction> transactions) {
        transactions.parallel()
            .filter(t -> !t.getStatus().equals("pending"))
            .forEach(this::union);
    }

    private String find(String transactionId) {
        parent.putIfAbsent(transactionId, transactionId);

        if (!parent.get(transactionId).equals(transactionId)) {
            long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
            parent.computeIfPresent(transactionId, (k, v) -> find(v));
            disjointnessCheckTime.addAndGet(System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime);
        }

        return parent.get(transactionId);
    }

    private void union(Transaction transaction) {
        String root1 = find(transaction.getId());
        String root2 = find(transaction.getRelatedTransactionId());

        if (!root1.equals(root2)) {
            parent.put(root1, root2);
        }
    }

    public long getDisjointnessCheckTime() {
        return disjointnessCheckTime.get();
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a ConcurrentHashMap for the Union-Find data structure to ensure thread safety and efficient concurrent access. The find method is used to identify the root of each transaction, while the union method merges two transactions into one set if they are not already in the same set. 

The disjointness check time is logged using AtomicLong for thread safety during concurrent updates. This allows you to monitor the performance and resource usage of your system under varying loads.

Please note that this is a simplified example and does not include error handling or other production-level considerations such as logging, exception handling, and more complex transaction processing logic. 

Also, please remember that Java's ConcurrentHashMap implementation uses fine-grained locking for high concurrency, which can lead to contention if there are many threads trying to modify the same entry in the map. If you find this is a bottleneck, you may need to consider using a more efficient data structure or partitioning your data across multiple maps.
']

