["Implementing a full-featured memory management library in C under resource-constrained conditions, including concurrency safety and data partitioning across multiple nodes, is a complex task that requires careful design and implementation. Here's an example of how you might start implementing such a library. 

Please note that this code is just a basic demonstration and does not include all the features mentioned in your requirements. It also lacks error handling for simplicity. In a real-world scenario, you would need to add proper error checking and handle edge cases.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int key;
    int value;
} Record;

typedef struct {
    long* offsets;
    int* keys;
    Record** records;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} DataStructure;

DataStructure* initialize(int size) {
    DataStructure* ds = malloc(sizeof(DataStructure));
    if (ds == NULL) return NULL;

    ds->offsets = calloc(size, sizeof(long));
    ds->keys = calloc(size, sizeof(int));
    ds->records = calloc(size, sizeof(Record*));
    pthread_mutex_init(&ds->lock, NULL);

    return ds;
}

void insert(DataStructure* ds, int key, int value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&ds->lock);

    // Find an empty slot in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(ds->keys)/sizeof(int); i++) {
        if (ds->keys[i] == 0) {
            ds->keys[i] = key;

            // Allocate memory for the record and initialize it
            Record* record = malloc(sizeof(Record));
            record->key = key;
            record->value = value;
            ds->records[i] = record;

            break;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ds->lock);
}

Record* lookup(DataStructure* ds, int key) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&ds->lock);

    // Find the record with the given key
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(ds->keys)/sizeof(int); i++) {
        if (ds->keys[i] == key) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&ds->lock);
            return ds->records[i];
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ds->lock);
    return NULL;
}
```
This is a very basic example and does not include all the features you mentioned, such as data partitioning across multiple nodes or memory management. Implementing these features would require a significant amount of additional code and design decisions. 

