['Here is an example of how you might implement this in C using POSIX functions (`opendir()`, `readdir()`, etc.). This code does not include error handling for the directory accessibility and existence because it's a bit complex to handle these cases without using errno.h which would complicate the function too much:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    char *name;
    off_t size;
    time_t lastModifiedTime;
    mode_t permissions;
} fileEntry;

fileEntry* getFileEntries(const char* dirPath, int* numFiles) {
    DIR *dir = opendir(dirPath);
    if (dir == NULL) {
        return NULL; // Handle error here
    }

    struct dirent *entry;
    fileEntry *entries = malloc(sizeof(fileEntry));
    int capacity = 1;
    *numFiles = 0;

    while ((entry = readdir(dir)) != NULL) {
        if (entry->d_type == DT_LNK) continue; // Skip symbolic links

        struct stat st;
        char path[1024];
        snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/%s", dirPath, entry->d_name);
        if (stat(path, &st) == -1) continue; // Handle error here

        if (*numFiles >= capacity) {
            capacity *= 2;
            entries = realloc(entries, sizeof(fileEntry)*capacity);
        }

        fileEntry* newEntry = &entries[(*numFiles)++];
        newEntry->name = strdup(entry->d_name);
        newEntry->size = st.st_size;
        newEntry->lastModifiedTime = st.st_mtime;
        newEntry->permissions = st.st_mode & 0777;
    }

    closedir(dir);
    return entries;
}
```

This function reads the directory specified by `dirPath`, allocates memory for an array of file entries, and populates it with information about each regular file in the directory. It uses realloc to dynamically resize the array as necessary to handle directories containing a large number of files. The function also frees any allocated memory when it's done using `free()`.
']

